WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.240
This vibration monitor uses an IRIV IO

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.779
Controller, running the Wilcoxon gateway firmware.

3
00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:08.832
It reads a Wilcoxon 883M, and

4
00:00:08.832 --> 00:00:11.663
publishes to Viking Analytics over MQTT.

5
00:00:12.883 --> 00:00:14.253
The sensor measures.

6
00:00:14.253 --> 00:00:16.856
The controller polls it and publishes.

7
00:00:16.856 --> 00:00:19.321
Viking Analytics learns the machine,

8
00:00:19.321 --> 00:00:20.897
and flags what changes.

9
00:00:22.117 --> 00:00:24.116
This simplified cutaway reveals the

10
00:00:24.116 --> 00:00:26.058
bearings, shaft and pump impeller.

11
00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:29.143
Forces from rotating parts travel through the bearings

12
00:00:29.143 --> 00:00:32.227
into the housing, where the sensor measures vibration.

13
00:00:32.227 --> 00:00:34.398
The motion is slowed for illustration;

14
00:00:34.398 --> 00:00:35.769
this is not a drawing of

15
00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:37.197
a specific motor or pump.

16
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.400
The 883M sits on the motor, and

17
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.581
measures vibration on three axes, plus temperature.

18
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:46.617
The controller polls it over Modbus,

19
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:48.804
and publishes each reading as MQTT.

20
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.808
MultiViz learns what normal looks like.

21
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:56.050
The Wilcoxon 883M measures vibration in

22
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.007
X, Y and Z, plus temperature.

23
00:00:58.007 --> 00:01:01.856
It reports acceleration, velocity and displacement, along

24
00:01:01.856 --> 00:01:04.556
with waveforms and a frequency spectrum.

25
00:01:04.556 --> 00:01:07.189
Its acceleration range is plus or minus

26
00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:09.957
sixteen g, with a frequency response from

27
00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:10.902
2 Hz to 5 kHz.

28
00:01:10.902 --> 00:01:13.197
The compact housing is thirty five

29
00:01:13.197 --> 00:01:16.100
millimetres wide and twenty six millimetres

30
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.261
high, with an M eight connector.

31
00:01:18.261 --> 00:01:20.286
It sends digital readings over

32
00:01:20.286 --> 00:01:21.164
Modbus R T U.

33
00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:24.607
These measurements help track changes in a machine;

34
00:01:24.607 --> 00:01:28.522
diagnosis also needs its operating conditions and history.

35
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.597
Its RS485 pair lands on A

36
00:01:31.597 --> 00:01:33.370
and B, with ground on G.

37
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.659
The controller ends the bus, so

38
00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:37.875
the terminator switch goes on.

39
00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:41.269
A 24 V supply powers the controller,

40
00:01:41.269 --> 00:01:43.455
and the sensor shares the same rail.

41
00:01:45.083 --> 00:01:47.682
Install the firmware from the web installer.

42
00:01:47.682 --> 00:01:49.926
Then set the MQTT broker and the alarm

43
00:01:49.926 --> 00:01:52.584
limits in the controller's own web dashboard.

44
00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:56.224
MultiViz learns a baseline for each sensor,

45
00:01:56.224 --> 00:01:58.184
so there are no thresholds to tune.

46
00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.760
When the pattern shifts, it names the symptom,

47
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.336
and ranks which machine needs attention first.

48
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:05.512
Now the alarm.

49
00:02:05.512 --> 00:02:08.261
Output zero follows the dashboard alarm,

50
00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.842
and drives a 24 V relay

51
00:02:09.842 --> 00:02:11.491
for a beacon and buzzer.

52
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:14.165
A push button on input

53
00:02:14.165 --> 00:02:15.942
one acknowledges the alarm.

54
00:02:15.942 --> 00:02:17.982
Input zero can start a capture.

55
00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:20.850
Vibration on the motor climbs

56
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.020
past the alarm limit.

57
00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:26.117
After the delay, the controller trips the alarm.

58
00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:27.980
Output zero closes the relay,

59
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.458
and the beacon flashes.

60
00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:32.383
The operator presses acknowledge.

61
00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:34.338
While vibration is still high,

62
00:02:34.338 --> 00:02:35.575
the relay stays on.

63
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:38.027
Once it clears, the relay drops,

64
00:02:38.027 --> 00:02:39.653
and the alarm is ready again.

65
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:43.088
The rule runs on the controller.

66
00:02:43.088 --> 00:02:45.350
Trip on velocity or temperature, after

67
00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.076
a delay, and latch the relay.

68
00:02:47.076 --> 00:02:48.922
It drops only when the alarm is

69
00:02:48.922 --> 00:02:51.422
acknowledged, and the reading has cleared.

70
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.103
Sense, analyse, act.

71
00:02:54.103 --> 00:02:56.574
One sensor, one controller, and an

72
00:02:56.574 --> 00:02:58.972
alarm that someone has to answer.

